      body: [
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--left\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">northants breakfast, 800t</h2><p>Black pudding discs and sunlight rashers sizzling in a pan. A window breeze carries his excited plans to your contented counter perch. The lake, the market –– soapsud hands, a winking chain against his velvet neck.</p></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem-divider\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--right\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">hunter-gatherers</h2><p>Trainers christened by sidebank mud and puddle spatter as marketgoing four-bys and saloons blur by unminding. A rambler’s pace to better see, better feel the town around you slowly growing. Half-collapsed walls, outhouses first, their haphazard shored-up roofs and nooks like unmaintained memory, domain of schoolchildren, red foxes, painters. These give way, the village proper, brickwork charmed and honey handsome. All homes.</p><p>Stalls, transplanted beach huts in the square. Emil guides you leisured through, double back and back again, past gormless eyes in ice bath crates, blue plastic bands around thick heads of basil herbs, loaves ornate as temples browning in the sun. City balking at the ease of those first score greeting smiles, you’ve by lunchtime loosened and can manage nods, unfeigned pleasantries. After coffee, your flushed, puff-chested imitation of the vegetable crier doubles Emil over with his shopping, before the moustachioed trad wax terror outside a butcher’s leaves him clutching at your shoulder. Later clocked he must have known about it, but not with you. Then, you almost say ––</p><p>Directed by the effigy inside, chilled linoleum sanctuary, half-pipe screen like museum glass preserving bloody tapestries of shin and shank, its fat impressionistic marbling. A jouncing belly beneath leather aprons, guffaws, bird’s wing beating in the back, cleaved carcasses. Delights all prideful paper packaged, bowed with string and slotted colourful as hardbacks onto cardiac bookshelves in your groaning ribcage frame. Produce fashionistas, forearm cramping under responsible recycled bag mass. Bunting wind down cobbled alleys tastes briefly tricolour on sorbet lips, artisan iced treats' sweet sting on every other flavour lingering, winding back across the town and homeward.</p></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem-divider\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--left\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">ministry</h2><blockquote><p>Along the cool sequester’d vale of life<br>They kept the noiseless tenor of their way.</p><cite>Thomas Gray, ‘Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard’, 1751</cite></blockquote><p>A gardener at the entrance grove wary watches your approach, his sullied glove the trowel’s whetstone, edge run smooth across his palm, pagan wedding blade in preparation.</p><p>Puttering, a nineties hatchback past the uncemented farside wall. Through headstone rows this chalk path winds around a loose perimeter. Lichen bursts adorn the elder, slow-grown skin on stone, mossy fireworks commemorating anniversaries subterrain. You keep Emil encircling steps ahead; he stops and ponders longest those with prompt arithmetic. His hand rests upon the rough hewn limestone now and then, communion in kind. Some marble flat as New England snowpack, undented yet by downsized boots and body prints. Maybe never. Do they drink the bouquets’ unclaimed time?</p><p>Young shade settles in its moveable nest beneath a stately yew who weeps in lieu of extinct bloodlines, truant families. Maybe force of habit. Between benches sentried in memoriam a stout door gapes, inside cool and hushen as a cave. Above, sky tensing grey, stray droplets finding skin. <em>Take a look inside?</em></p><p><em>I’m not sure.</em></p><p>Light torqued faltering through outside boughs and glassy coloured scenes dances a slow disco up the aisle. Must, and candlewax, and pews on pews hung shin height with tiny tapestries, horsehair hassocks. The Tree of Life. Man and kid waist deep in water. Doves.</p><p><em>Saint Chris. That’s our boy.</em> A youthful vicar, bespectacled, hands clasped at navel nervously. <em>Carrying the Christ-child across the river.</em> Gesturing expectant at the kneeler.</p><p><em>My dad has his pendant on the rear view</em>, Emil offers. Priest’s teeth white as remembrance graves. <em>May he travel safely always.</em> He talks the architecture through you, rainfall rapping on the leaden roof, bedside manner surfing sonic wavelets through a cavernous nave. <em>Made for good acoustics, you see. Makes my shouting matches with God much more dramatic. </em> Involuntary grin across your trellised cheek. Smooth as conkers, poppyheads in fleur-de-lys sit familiar in your grasp, ancient holds at anchor for your voyaging gaze into dark wood vault above –– an upturned ark which rocks in awe or vertigo. Speakers bulge from stony recesses, a tripod camera for Zoom services chirping like an insect in the gangway. Precision in the masonry, labourers’ handprints all invisibly impressed on Roman columns, pointed arches, faith indivisible and brought to stone. Those bedraggled Saxon men an age and more ago hauling blocks of Barnack limestone up the hill from snorting horses’ carts, lunching on the scaffolding their crusts, a novel vista spanning green lea swathes, reading bird’s eye the river’s course beyond which spotted copses faint and livid as a thought.</p><p>At the altar, minutes melt in the outside torrent, heaven’s own tears leaking down the leadlights. A dream’s departing tail, it eases by degrees to utter stillness. You while a while away under heedful priestly scrutiny.</p><p><em>We’d best be off</em>, Emil smiles in time. Returned, and practised bow. Outside sodden, revived and somehow brilliant, a gloss across it all. Sky quiet, bright. Footsteps retraced, the seedsman doffs a dampened cap, misshapen toothy beaming. Deep creases in his leather skin, shamanic mudstreaks.</p><p><em>Thought that was quite good.</em> You see Emil apart, most still in that church’s pregnant silence. To have been here long before, remain long after, the timeless sound of metal parting dirt and smoothing.</p></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem-divider\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--right\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">rainfall interlude</h2><p>You sit at the cold threshold, drops like tears rolling from leaves overhead that fall in black and white on the sash sill beside. On the other, strings reverberate from the hollow beneath his fingers; the sound emerges, tentative, shoots winding up and out from the shade of a lattice. A sorrowful swing, singing through his broad ribcage. Ash blooms from your lit cigarette in charcoal petals.</p><p>They talk of loves lost in the low lamp glow and laugh, at the bounce of the pick. Strings subside, their thrum thick dissipating through roomspace to settle in sheets across settee and footstool. Over your skin. Shifting stormlines on a weatherdesk, coastal buffeting and out to sea, the farthest bands wring taut in fireplace heat and snap. Sound rebirthed as phoenix embers.</p><p>The other sighs. <em>It’s easy to say, isn’t it? We were a thing once. But that can never capture the size of the thing.</em></p><p>Emil, grasping the guitar’s neck, flicks a fishhook glance your way, eyes asmile.</p><p><em>There’s an impossibility in distilling that strength of feeling. It needs a sonnet. An epic. Music.</em></p><p>The other nodding absently. Emil’s fingers fit playfully down frets, soundless chords which ripple whispers at your neck, your thighs –– tactility recollected. Your tossed cig end lands on cobblestone, lone flame in twisting wind, studied till it vanishes. The raindrum slows a time, then fiercens.</p></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem-divider\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--left\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">paradise under a concrete overpass</h2><p>You dream of Eden. Your flattened hand at a spring’s masoned mouth, water’s course arrested, wriggling. Liquid palmistry. Smooth stones underfoot. Fitful downstream glisters jewel the waterweave, the crest of crossing currents braiding infinite and veiling shallow darting fish in glossy scale. You unstop the Green Man’s spout, wreathen face benign and ancient; his cooling issue lineates your limbs and cascades the contours of your naked body fountainesque.</p><p>Turning, ash trees dot corrugated country, up where blackened birds streak synchronised. Bells peal over in the sun, you think, and summon you to supper. A step into the pampas at the brook’s edge, flaxen stems drying as Mary’s hair your calves, your feet as you carve a path through virgin meadow: fingers grazing grassheads and warm hillock crowns of sleeping children on your upward climb. Spring gusts ease warm laundry scents crossgarden, splayed hanging cloths and linen on the washing line. Firm apple flesh abounding in a wicker basket. Scudding clouds unpatch the sun to unobstructed earthly stream, and you to kiss all over.</p><p>He calls you, nearby. Beyond that flower-riven ridge you’ll find him. Under the concrete overpass.</p></div>"
      ]
