      body: [
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--left\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">reedsong</h2><div class=\"wa-epigraph\"><em>after ‘The Lake Isle of Innisfree’ by W. B. Yeats</em></div><p>A vivid, sweat-stained tableau, rowers frozen in motion. The crook of your arms on his knees, and his heat behind, preening as ducklings do.</p><p>Warmth at the lake’s lip; sand sifts beneath your palms in the gentle tide, shoals of coarse and tiny fish. Warm wash at your midriff. Emil cups handfuls over your scalp, running down the contours of your back. Silt billows the shallows as you rake, cloudling mist over moors at night-time.</p><p><em>I feel like John the Baptist.</em> His laugh skims over the water plain.</p><p><em>Would that make me Jesus, then?</em></p><p>Glancing behind, his profile, granite cast against cornflower canvas, white teeth cutting wide blue. Emil whips a hand across the sheen flatness, snapping surface gems which flash, brilliant. They arc closer to your eyes.</p><p>Blink. Run a reed between your fingers till it sings, eyelids sink. Enjoy his touch, your skin getting warm all at once. <em>Or any one of the thousand other nameless fuckers.</em> Guided into a bow, damp pads of fingers trace invisible shapes from the nub at your neck, languages learnt and lost between waterline and skin. His hand immersed again, your spinal ridges bind the yet blank back pages where characters curl and dry.</p><p>That low grey drone, somewhere out beyond rustling watergrass and goose-calls. Regular, gridlock, machine murmurs, aftershocks sending square ripples across the surface. <em>I can still hear it</em>, you say. <em>Even here.</em></p><p>His hands on your shoulders now, pulling softly, firmly, till you sink easy to his chest. Hands cup your ears, warm as conches, and all you hear is the water’s whisper. Orange crescents at your eyelids all you can see. His kick-drum heart bounds through your head, like the beat of a bird’s wing. Your skin glimmers with proximity and lakeshine. Your body buoyed by his. <em>Peace comes slow.</em></p><p>Emil’s hands unmuffle you. You hear small waves breaking at the shore. The lakescape brightens, sharpens, boatmen skitting nervous in the shadow of dragonflies. Darkened ruins crag the fell’s far peak.</p><p><em>You don’t have to go back.</em></p></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem-divider\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--right\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">haecceity</h2><blockquote><p>The meadows never enough of freshening water;<br>Love never seems to have enough of tears.</p><cite>Virgil, <em>Eclogue X</em>, trans. David Ferry</cite></blockquote><p><em>Say hello to the old man for me.</em> His hand entwined with yours, playful fingers courting swanlike, knuckles genuflect, against each others’ nudging ritually. The other twists beach bag cord upon his shoulder, faintly stained in engine oil from the breakdown still. <em>Probably unwise.</em> Brand your eardrums with his laugh’s frequency, bottle its curve amongst the chatter.</p><p>A small shift absents skin for gorilla glass, thumbing up your e-ticket as the stagecoach’s slate windscreen slab swings into view. Half-turn: how to say what underpins it all beneath a shelter birdshit spattered, beneath foliage in summer glory. <em>Bye, Emil.</em> His smiling sorrow, beads in crow’s feet nooked, offers in his irises until the last. You turn and board.</p></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem-divider\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--left\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">pater</h2><p>Down to kneel at the fireplace, split trunks toted in his spotted forearms positioned flower-careful in the hearth. Matchhead rips, shadow playing in his cavern wrinkles, keening eyes upon the flame, armoured by his palm until it kindles ruckled rag. How he shakes.</p><p>He shoos away your offered help, labours aching to his feet, grunts into his chair. More like a swaddle now than throne.</p><p>Instinctive arm at three o’clock toward the crystalline decanter. <em>So. I know the timings of these visits now. How is that friend of yours?</em> Something like a challenge in his gaze.</p></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem-divider\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--right\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">a blooding premature</h2><p>A web spread wide between gilded frame and topmost corner of the room, tenant curled. The same upon the pastel walls as beyond –– game bound or beating across tumbling vista pale.</p><p>Years past. Raw knees knocking and one eye globed by magnified glass, documentarian gianted in study of Wellington boots reeling forth from which a colony of ants parades flat across Dutch tiled terrain.</p><p>Hung mud and coalsmoke daily shifting on frescoed walls and in footwells, you’re alive to every component. Woodland alchemy. Stalking hallways houndlike, by subtle scents bewitched. Sarcococca breeze and daffodil, the spring herald. Acrid shot and leather wintered at the tradesman’s entrance. Those short yule evenings. The dun circus: trenchcoats stained and oily ragging through, dogyip and engines howling in the yard, skipping under men in figure eights.</p><p>Dual door barrel bursts more entry and hemmed in, instinctive, you catch a pheasant’s head to clamour, your father’s roar like shortwave radio. Your beak agape, its striking eye returns your stare, amber bedded in rouge well. Warm and leaking aster. Hands puppeted by larger others, the head plummets. Cheeks besmudged with warm and warlike stripes: embarrassment burns a quiet fury, eardrums pounding, left deaf as the troupe aways to spirits fireside. Blood pooling at the corner of your mouth. On your fingers. Ants encircle a grand new offering, terracotta platter. The revelry begins its lurch into the early hours so through the brimming dark you scamper holloways across the lawn, still marked, al fresco earthly cradles, barken priestholes waiting there –– dug in to nap amongst the solace sprites.</p><p>Some hours later, thickened voices thin on the wind. Amidst scrubland you shiver silent till they dwindle, him returned to stupored armchair visions. The door unbolted rasps ajar, and all above drawn portraits stretching indistinct in bland gallery. Those long winter nights.</p></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem-divider\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"wa-poem wa-poem--left\"><h2 class=\"reading__section\">flendysshe</h2><blockquote><p>What I thought was the first star turned out to be the night light for a plane coming into Luton.</p><cite>Robert Macfarlane, <em>The Old Ways: A Journey on Foot</em>, 2012</cite></blockquote><p>When the sky frowns at dusk, you scuttle gravel up dad’s twilit lane, with every stride the past about you ossifying. Undergrowth whispers the day’s secrets, thistle and hush. A wan light sinks between the trees. Latch-click and creak, close. The gate swings slow, carving its phantom arc continuous with, moving through, unmoving greyness. Day’s hard light leaks from chipping mortar, cracks in dry stone wall, misting down at vision’s limits, softening things into themselves. This someplace, elven scene, all parsed myth and ashen. Ken stretched thin.</p><p>When the night draws in, young, and the forest beyond the hollow’s limits darkens, and comes alive. Childhood folklore bristling at the edgeland, Scots pine feet. Bright-eyed horror: that wyrm in the outbuilding bog flicking again an electric tongue at your plimsolled heels. That sow’s skull, wrapped in hazard tape and memory. Dug up from three feet deep but garbled lengthwise along the village mile; so came to pass mass murder in Flendysshe, and wild boar out dusken rooting. There are hundreds of these places where the earth is tangled haystacks and bonfires’ charred sootprints –– every footfall further cracks Playmobil limbs and shards of vodka bottlenecks, their peeling labels. Perimeter trees adorned with fairy lights, long fatigued but still aglow, ghoulish under clear full moons. Wayward nails on fenceposts are the trailsigns, if only you could read the rust. Of the owl-king’s fortnight dominion they would speak, his mighty works and treetop palace skipped, unspared: the filthy long sleeve tee, erstwhile royal standard daubed in ready-mix primary paints, still shivers in the branches. They know of botched allotments, warn of boggarts in the compost heaps whose laughter howls through rubber piping, and hung on them are dog-chewed trainers, lacquerboards with misspelled portents, disregarded bras from teenage carport lovemaking through long red evenings. You can breathe here. These muddy jigsaw tracts, weedy rus, ramshackle urbe, the last outpost of fable. And sharing all a tainted sky specked there by stars or static satellites. Standing still, the movements beyond the hedgerow start up again once more, unseen.</p></div>"
      ]
