      body: [
        "It is a glorious summer’s day, and the boy makes the most of the hard track in front of him to run and run. Those few clouds above in the pale sky hang lazily, like soapsuds in lukewarm water. He can almost feel the dirt beneath his thin rubber plimsolls, and he desperately wishes to take them off and tread upon the ground proper; but he refrains, because Mother said not yet, he must wait til the beach; not yet, one never knows what sharp and unpleasant thing one’s foot might come down on.",
        "His footfalls have taken him up a steady incline and he slows and spins, hands planted proudly on either hip. He must look like Cortés in the Darién, planting his stick in the topmost peak. There is a satisfying burn at the tops of his calves that reminds him of his efforts, and his chest rises and falls rapidly beneath his linen shirt. Below him, a way back along the path, he can make out the five figures that form the rest of his party, and the two darting, dancing dogs that bounce excitedly at their heels. Ging Ging leads the motley circus, almost tripping herself over in the tangled leads and shrieking merrily, her impossible hat sitting atop her head at an impossible angle. Behind her is Mother, face angled towards him on the crest of the hill, features hidden in shadow by her wide-brimmed sun hat. He can tell that she is smiling. Following them comes Nanny, carrying the boy’s inventory of games, and trailing some way behind are Lewis and Watts, each struggling with the weight of a huge wicker picnic basket. He laughs at the ridiculous procession, before raising his hand in a wave. ‘Come along, everyone!’ he cries, as a warm gust of wind blows in from behind, ruffling his thatch hair across his face. He turns, and, brushing his fringe away, looks down the hill and realises that the land runs out.",
        "The sky sinks into the earth just a few hundred feet ahead of him. Baffled, he stares at mirage a moment, mouth agape, until he realises that what had looked like little clouds appearing and disappearing before reappearing once more were crests of waves, swash that rises and falls before spreading out onto the sand like warm butter.",
        "It is the sea.",
        "John has seen drawings of it in his picture books, and heard stories about it from Father in a telegram about his travels to the Middle East, as well as from Watts, who had served in the Navy during the Great War and lost a finger in one of the vast metal doors of a battleship, but never knew it could be so vast. It looks so still, so immovable, yet to focus on any one point is to see that it is ever moving, surf crowns on waves that flash like white grins against the blue; how it shimmers as though hiding beneath the surface is an open treasure chest, or Mother’s box of pearls that she keeps in her dressing table that John would carefully move, and sit cross legged on the quilt of the four-poster bed, rolling the cloudy marbles between his fingers.",
        "He starts as Ging Ging’s shrill voice calls out from behind. ‘Look, my darling, what a view, what a view!’ she says in French, tottering up to the peak to stand beside him. The dogs roam and sniff excitedly around the boy’s legs, their damp black noses and whiskers tickling him. Between them and the sea, the land stretches off to both sides in undulating sand dunes dotted here and there with barbed wire and tufts of reed and gorse, pale green patches amongst the beach sands. Across the bay, far, far over to their right the pastel houses of the village look pale and miniature, like John’s wooden playset on the rug in the nursery. A few scatter lazily up the side of the hill, reaching just above the spire of the church before giving way to wild outcroppings of rock. ‘It is quite beautiful, Ging Ging,’ he replies quietly, in her native tongue. Mother appears beside him, her yellow summer dress billowing in the breeze. She brings his head gently to her side so that it rests just above her hip, and caresses the conch of his ear with her forefinger. ‘What do you make of that, John, darling?’ she asks softly. Before he can reply, however, Pippa yips suddenly and scrabbles at the boy’s shorts.",
        "‘Down,’ John says commandingly. His attention, though, is drawn to Ging Ging’s feet, whose bright red toenails shine like ten tiny buoys. John swings his face up towards Mother and points accusingly, wordlessly. She smiles, and gives him a small nod. The boy rips off his plimsolls, casting them over his shoulder in abandon, and makes the most of the softening path in front of him to run and run towards the vast blue."
      ]
